  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled and running. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The index has about 14,000 web requests, with timestamps from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. Each request records:\n- **When:** `timestamp`, `hour_of_day`\n- **Response code:** `response`, a text field. Only three codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n- **Size:** `bytes`\n- **Who:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`\n- **What:** `host`, `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **Where:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-4b5125ba Web logs overview\"** (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4b5125ba-web-logs-overview-d0d48c25ca1a) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nThe dashboard opens on the last 7 days by default.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-4b5125ba 5xx spike\"** (id `77c8da5d-47da-4bec-aa3d-a64e5520cbe9`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Every minute it counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" and fires when more than 10 arrived in the last 5 minutes. It is tagged `EFF-4b5125ba` and `web-logs`.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it creates an alert in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone. Add a connector such as Slack or email if you want notifications.",
